Also spelled ''apologise.''

'''apologizer''' (a̤-pol′ọ̄-jī-zėr), ''n.'' One who apologizes; one who makes apologies or excuses. Also spelled ''apologiser.''

'''apologue''' (ap′ọ̄-log), ''n.'' [< F. ''apologue,'' < L. ''apologus,'' < Gr. ἀπόλογος, a story, tale, fable, < ἀπό, from, + λέγειν, speak, λόγος, speech.] A story or relation of fictitious events intended to convey useful truths; a moral fable; an allegory. {{smaller|An ''apologue'' differs from a ''parable'' in that the latter is drawn from events which occur among mankind, and is therefore supported by probability, while the former may be founded on supposed actions of brutes or inanimate things, and therefore does not require to be supported by probability. Æsop’s fables are good examples of apologues.}}

'''apologuer†''' (ap′ọ̄-log-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''apologue'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who writes apologues; a fabler. ''Burton.''

'''apology''' (a̤-pol′ọ̄-ji), ''n.''; pl. ''apologies'' (-jiz). [= F. ''apologie,'' < LL. ''apologia,'' < Gr. ἀπολογία, a speech in defense, < ἀπολογεῖσθαι, speak in defense: see ''apologetic,'' and cf. ''apologue.''] '''1.''' Something said or written in defense, vindication, or excuse; specifically, a defense or justification of a doctrine, system, course of conduct, etc., against objections or criticisms.
{{smaller block|I shall neither trouble the reader nor myself with any ''apology'' for publishing these sermons.
{{right|''Tillotson.''}}
Bishop Watson’s “''Apology'' for the Bible” is a good book with a bad title.
{{right|''R. Hall.''}}}}
'''2.''' An excuse, usually accompanied by an expression of regret, for some fault.
{{smaller block|''Apologies'' only account for what they do not alter.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli.''}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''3.''' That which imperfectly serves a given purpose; a temporary substitute; a makeshift.
{{smaller block|He wears a wisp of black silk round his neck, without any stiffener, as an ''apology'' for a neckerchief. {{right|''Dickens.''}}}}
'''4†.''' An apologue.
{{smaller block|A pretty ''apology'' of a league that was made betwixt the wolves and the sheep.
{{right|''Topsell'', Four-Footed Beasts, p. 578. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn. 2.''' ''Apology, Excuse, Plea.'' ''Apology'' has in this sense the force of an admission that one has been, at least seemingly, in the wrong; it therefore pleads any extenuating circumstances, or, more often, offers a frank acknowledgment as the best that can be done toward making matters right. ''Excuse'' may mean a defense, or an explanation simply: as, his ''excuse'' was quite sufficient; or it may be a mere attempt at justification: as, it was only an ''excuse;'' or it may be a begging to be released from a claim: as, “they all with one consent began to make ''excuse,''” Luke xiv. 18. A ''plea'' consists, according to the occasion, of an appeal for leniency, or of justificatory or exculpatory argument or persuasion.}}
{{smaller block|Our English Martyrologer counted it a sufficient ''apology'' for what meanness might be found in the first edition of his “Acts and Monuments,” that it was “hastily rashed up in about fourteen months.”
{{right|''C. Mather'', Introd. to Mag. Chris.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Weakness is thy ''excuse'',
And I believe it. {{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 829.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|Hellenic art and philosophy were and remain an unconscious ''plea'' for humanity in its own right.
{{right|''Faiths of the World'', p. 301.}}}}

